re less about, um, killing anything big or I don't, I could care less about story. Like, you know, someone sends a photo of, oh, look at this giant buck or bull I killed. I could, I could really care less. Um, what I am interested in is like the stories and the adventure. Um, so it's like, what, what went into killing that fricking 200 inch buck? You know, like, is it, uh, you just go out on opening day and have a super easy stock with your bow and shoot it or a rifle hunt or whatever. And here's the photo of the animal. Like, I just, I'm completely numb to it. I'm like, I don't, okay, cool. Great. Um, but if you have like this adventure, the, in the story and this memory made, that's what I resonate with. Um, so maybe for this guy, he needs to reevaluate like why, um, I think I've kind of talked about this, like with, in relation to the kids, like, why am I hunting? Um, what are the things that I enjoy about it? Um, and for me, it's either, it really just falls into like going out. I mean, obviously just, just love being, and I was, um, completely and utterly reminded of this Alaska because it was so busy and stressful in August. And then the second we got out there, uh, not the second, took a couple of days to like, just be present in the mountains and just stop thinking about work and everything going on. Um, but man, I mean, there's something certainly, um, cathartic and, and just like, you know, it's like going to church when you're out there, man. Like you're just, it's just peaceful, um, refreshing, resetting, come back to regular life with renewed energy. Um, but also I'm out there for adventure, uh, and, and to make memories. Um, and I'm out there to bring meat home to the family. Uh, so those are, those are my why, you know, my reasons why I'm hunting. And maybe for this guy, he just needs to reevaluate that. Maybe like he might be more where I'm at. Like, I don't care if this buck is 180 or 220. I really, really, really don't care. Um, and I know that's, 