o nice. So do you have any regrets? No, zero. Absolutely zero regrets. There's no, I mean, from, I like that he mentioned the distances that he's going to shoot at. That's a huge part of all this, right? If you're someone who's like, yeah, I'm going to shoot animals a thousand yards, then I think that one, you question that too. Then you, you know, start these little things, maybe make a difference where you get the added velocity. I mean, they certainly will make a difference in your ballistics and how much wind drift you have. But for practical hunting ranges, you know, 500 yards out to 600 yards, whether you have a 22-inch barrel or 16-inch barrel, you're talking, you know, very, very minimal differences in worst-case scenarios. So I have zero regret going to 16. I think recently, you're now with the new stuff, you can go even shorter, maybe. Yeah, with basically like the changes with NFA and SBRs, more guys are going shorter because of the process of having a technical short barrel rifle is easier. Yeah, easier. Yes. Gotcha. In my mind, the reason I want a short barrel and a folding chassis is so when the rifle is on my pack, maybe a little bit like when it's in your hand, it's easier to maneuver, but it's also, it's in your hand and it's already easy to maneuver. So whether it's 16-inch barrel or 22-inch barrel, like these aren't like make or break things. But if you're hunting really brushy country and, you know, I mean, anyone who's, you know, hunted Alaska, Kodiak, whatever, with a long barrel, long suppressor, it's freaking miserable. You get stuck in alders all day long. And so that was like the first, you know, catalyst of like, okay, I need something different. This, this is miserable. And I was like, like our first trip to Kodiak in 2018 or 19, I can't remember what it was. Very shortly after that, the next time we went back, so I had a freaking folding chassis and they'd also just kind of come out at that time. But that solves that problem. Like, and so right now for me, I'm running my mountain gear chassis with a 16-inch barrel and the airlock, whether, you know, their five-inch suppressor, the gravity, zero gravity one on the six millimeter. And I have the nano on the 22. That is mounted on the side of the pack and it's like completely within the profile of the pack. It's not sticking up higher than my backpack. So with the, therefore it's not getting caught on more brush. So I had like a friend who's like chopped his 16-inch down to 12 already. And I was like, honestly, that's just like a point of diminishing returns. Like it's already low enough that I'm not getting hung up. I've already solved the problem. I can't further solve the problem. I go even shorter. You guys see, save a little bit more weight, but I'm, I think I need to add weight to my rifle to make it more accurate at longer ranges. So that's not really a benefit. But, uh, so to me, 16-inch barrel, four or five-inch suppressor, it's like the perfect sweet spot. Uh, that's, uh, and I'll say that that's me with a 25-inch frame. Going back to frame, my low lifters earlier discussion, um, that, uh, if I was short and run a 23-inch, half-inch frame, maybe I would want it a little bit shorter. Um, I, in my opinion for me and what the distance is that I shoot big game at, uh, none of it really has any impact on my ability to make clean ethical shots and, and, you know, accurate shots, whether it's 20, 16, or 12 inches. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah, I agree. I don't have any, any regrets. I mean, in the past, uh, 10 years, I've gone from 24-inch barrel to a 22-inch barrel to a 20-inch barrel to an 18-inch barrel and also have a 16-inch barrel. Anything in, for me, that 16 to 18-inch range is fantastic. Uh, I'm not, like, in a hurry to change out my 18s, but in general, if I'm getting something new, it's going to be 16, 16.5, uh, because some barrels are still just default cut to 16.5, for example. Um, but yeah, 16 to 18-inch range with a folding chassis and, like you said, a 4 to 5-inch suppressor has been just the sweet spot of capable, uh, plenty capable, terminally capable, um, keeping speeds out to the distances that I want to shoot at. And just packing around, fantastic. Um, yeah, that's just been the sweet spot. So, I'm, I'm with you, Steve, of, like, yeah, we have buddies going shorter and tinkering and trying and whatever, and it's like, oh, that's cool. I kind of don't fully for, like, see the benefit of it, not the point of it, because it's, like, fun to try stuff and have this crazy compact little rifle. Um, you know, and maybe if that's this little, you know, scenarios, everything, right? Like, guys may be doing it for a purpose, but for my purpose, for the way I hunt, for being on the back, for backpack hunting, um, something in that range we just talked about. I just don't see it getting better. Yeah. And that's, uh, we're assuming we're putting a 4 or 5-inch suppressor on that. So, we're ending around a 20, 21-inch kind of effective barrel length. Um, so if you're not going to run a suppressor, then, yeah, I'd say go 20. Um, the longer, the better, just because it's going to only increase your velocity, um, and give you better ballistics, uh, if that's the only metric you're looking at, right? Yep. Yeah, and for guys, again, if you want to take a deeper dive to this, if you're newer to rifles and maybe don't understand all the variables, there are other things that we didn't accommodate for in this conversation. With length, for example, like density altitude, it can make a difference if you're hunting at sea level versus 5,000 feet versus 10,000 feet. So just, you know, if you want the best broad picture of truly calculating what type of speeds do I need at the muzzle for this bullet, for this, you know, downrange impact velocity, your max hunting distance, and then equating that to barrel length and how short can you go. So if you want to, again, kind of do that math, it is essentially a math problem, uh, with