, Nick died. And I'm like, Nick, who? And then realized, and then you said in a crash. I'm like, Oh crap. So the Nick Peters, the pilot who's flown us on three different hunts, not five days after we came out of the field with him, was taken off with a moose hunter. And I don't, we don't have much of the details yet other than, uh, the plane crashed and both he and the hunter passed away. And that was just, um, it was a lot, man. He was a fricking awesome guy. Really enjoyed communicating with him. You know, you got to spend, I mean, not, not going to call him like a, uh, he was a friend. Um, I was like, I didn't know him exceptionally well. Just, you know, he's in his mid thirties, young kids loved what he did. Um, thoroughly, thoroughly enjoyed our conversations flying in and out of the field. Every time we were with him. Um, you know, I would text him randomly off and on, you know, uh, throughout the years. Um, and yeah, to, to hear that he passed and, um, it being so, you know, from a selfish perspective, it was like, it was just so like we, I just shook his hand five days prior. Um, and thanked him for the flight and said, can't wait to see you again. And, uh, to hear that news, man, it was tough and definitely held my kids extra long that night. Um, have been every, every night since he just, he never, I mean, it's so easy to, um, get caught up in daily life where you're just, you know, like stressed and struggling and you're busy and everything just seems to be going by so fast. And then all of a sudden, and it, it's also easy to, not easy, but you can say like, oh, you need to like live with passion and, and every day could be your last, which is a very true statement that comes to a harsh reality. When something like, like this happens, um, and reality for me, I mean, there, there has to be some balance. Like you can't, you can't live so carefree every day. Cause it might be your last that you're like, that you're not working and moving forward and making m