st. But sometimes I feel like guys make more bad shots because they're misjudging distance. He said, I'm of the opinion that I would rather lose an opportunity because I couldn't get an exact range than just guess a range and hope I get the distance right when I take a shot. He says, I feel like I remember you guys touching on this topic at some point, but I can't find the podcast. What are your guys thoughts on this? Yeah. Yeah. We talked about that some part of this year. I think, um, I'm absolutely of his opinion, uh, unless it's just slam dunk. Like I know it's 30 yards and under, you know, it's like, it's just, it's dead. Uh, if it's, um, maybe I answered this slightly different, but I would say like, once I kind of go past 30 yards, I'm going to like a hundred percent, I'm ranging every single time. And I think if you look, you know, it's very, very easy to get caught in the moment of that shot. Like very, like, oh my gosh, animals there. I need to shoot now. Uh, I would say from experience and you could probably ask a thousand hunters this. And if they're honest, they probably like you, you always have more time than you think. You just put the pressure on. I need to shoot now. Um, and you got to get out of that mindset. Cause that is what creates errors, right? Like you're just, you're not only not ranging, but you're then rushing the shot, like draw anchor anchor, shoot, you know, like real, everything's happening so fast. It happens in a blur. You need to like take that extra. It's like a half a second, man, to like slow down, process everything. Um, obviously ranging takes longer than half a second, but it's just like a little bit of, uh, put the brakes on, assess everything, make sure you're good. And you, you always have that extra second or two to make a good shot. Um, so yeah, past 30 yards I'm ranging. And then, oh yeah, where I was going with this is in an individual hunt, I could certainly see, you could argue like, oh, I didn't have time to range.