nd low light situations. And that's the speed at which it ranges. There's, there's little things there that you kind of help improve your chances to, to get a range and make that shot. Yeah. When you said an old range finder, I just had a flashback and remember that I bought a  Me selling you the Nikon 20 years ago. That's what I was going to say. It was like a Nikon and it was in like Realtree Max camo. If it's a Nikon 440, I'm going to, I feel, I don't know why. I know it was like Realtree Max 1 camo. Nikon. Nikon, Nikon 440 range finder. Holy crap. I used to buy hand me down all the time, by the way. The Marmot tent, old Nikon range finder. That's funny. Yeah. That is funny. I can't remember what I ate for breakfast yesterday, but I can remember the range finder model number that I sold you 20 years ago. Make that make sense. Right. Yeah. I, I'm with you on that. Like I would summarize and say, if you don't range consistently, like you only range when it's fully convenient. You think you have the time. If you don't range consistently, you will shoot more elk. If you range consistently, you will kill and recover more elk. So it's like, I mean, that is what it is. If you take the curve, like you were talking about sample size, if you take your elk hunting career and you're an archery elk hunter for however many years, and let's say you did hunt archery elk every year. Do you want to shoot more elk or do you want to kill and recover more elk? That's the question. And ranging is going to help you do one and not do the other. Yeah. Yeah. I completely agree. All right. Let's wrap it there, Steve. We will be back. We said this before, but we will with more Monday minutes, more podcasts. We've got some great stories scheduled with listeners. We've got Brian Sheep Hunt who won the experience challenge. We're talking with him. We will get around to sharing the story of our Alaska trip. There's a whole bunch more coming. And if you guys have stories or photos or anything li